  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "The authors assembled a cross-cultural dataset of multilayer social networks across 50 communities in 29 countries, covering diverse livelihoods including fishers, pastoralists, horticulturalists, farmers, and wage laborers. Household-level surveys recorded ten types of material and immaterial support exchanges to construct inter-unit social networks. Comparing network summary statistics and nomination patterns across these communities and against benchmark datasets reveals high structural heterogeneity.",
      "why": "This dataset provides empirical baseline metrics for non-industrialized and culturally diverse populations, challenging universal assumptions and standard rules of thumb regarding human social network structure."
    }
